= Solution
{parent=d}

\b[False.] The preceding interpolation argument proves that the event $\{B_t/t\to0\}$ has <probability> one. Its complementary event, which is the nonconvergence assertion printed here, therefore has <probability> zero. The normalization by $t$ dominates both the integer-time increments and the fluctuations within each unit interval.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

\b[False.] For every deterministic $t>0$, <Brownian scaling> gives $B_t/\sqrt t\sim N(0,1)$. In particular, for any $\varepsilon>0$,
$$
\mathbb P(|B_t|/\sqrt t>\varepsilon)=2\bigl(1-\Phi(\varepsilon)\bigr)>0,
$$
independently of $t$. Almost-sure convergence would imply <convergence in probability>, but these fixed positive tail probabilities rule out convergence in <probability> to zero. The square-root normalization retains fluctuations of order one.

= f
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

\b[True.] The preceding distributional argument alone shows that almost-sure convergence to zero cannot hold with <probability> one. To establish almost-sure nonconvergence, rather than merely failure of a probability-one convergence claim, use the <Kolmogorov zero-one law>.

Let $A=\{B_n/\sqrt n\to0\}$. For every fixed $m$, subtracting $B_m/\sqrt n\to0$ shows that
$$
A=\left\{\frac{\sum_{k=m+1}^n(B_k-B_{k-1})}{\sqrt n}\longrightarrow0\right\}.
$$
Thus $A$ belongs to the <tail sigma-algebra> of the independent unit-time increments; it is a <tail event>. The <Kolmogorov zero-one law> gives $\mathbb P(A)\in\{0,1\}$. If its <probability> were one, then $B_n/\sqrt n\to0$ in <probability>, contrary to its standard <normal distribution> at every $n$. Therefore $\mathbb P(A)=0$. Continuous-time convergence would imply integer-time convergence, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\bigl(B_t/\sqrt t\text{ does not converge to }0\bigr)=1.}
$$
The more general <Brownian fluctuations exceed the square-root scale> result is consistent with this direct tail-event proof. No <independence> of the normalized values themselves has been assumed.

